Addressing plan
Topology Arrangement Advantages Disadvantages
Bus All nodes share one 
common backbone cable 
with terminators at both 
ends.
Simple; least cabling; 
cheap.
One cable fault stops 
the whole network; hard 
to troubleshoot.
Star Every node connects to a 
central hub/switch.
Easy to add/remove 
nodes; single node 
fault does not affect 
others.
Central device failure 
downs the whole 
network; more cable.
Ring Nodes form a closed loop; 
data passes node to node.
Orderly, predictable 
data flow; no collisions 
with token passing.
A single break can halt 
the ring; adding nodes 
disrupts it.
Mesh Every node links directly to 
every other node.
Highly reliable; 
multiple paths; faulttolerant.
Very expensive; huge 
amount of cabling.
Tree Groups of star networks 
joined to a bus backbone 
(hierarchy).
Scalable; good for 
large structured 
networks.
Backbone failure 
affects large sections; 
complex.
Hybrid Combination of two or 
more of the above.
Flexible; tailored to 
needs.
Design and 
management are 
complex and costly.

